# Prelude No. 2 in C minor, Well-Tempered Clavier I, BWV 847 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a toccata. Both hands run the same sixteenth-note figure
# (a turn round one note, then a leap) through a slow chord progression,
# relentless and driven, like Prelude No. 1 in a hurry. Near the end
# the motor breaks down and Bach writes three tempo marks himself:
# Presto (bar 28), a one-bar Adagio recitative (bar 34) and Allegro
# (bars 35-38) for the close.
#
# Time signature and bars: 4/4, 38 bars, no upbeat. The Mutopia MIDI
# already carries the three marks as tempo changes (quarter 120, then
# 160 at bar 28, 40 at bar 34, 96 at bar 35), which confirms the bar
# numbers. Our tempo command replaces all of them, so they are rebuilt
# below with broaden.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 100 for the opening Allegro-like motion. Track times
# (MusicBrainz track lists, whole prelude including the Presto and
# Adagio): Sviatoslav Richter 1:18 and Edwin Fischer 1:17 (both about
# 118 for the main section), Angela Hewitt (Hyperion, 1998) 1:31 (about
# 101), Rosalyn Tureck 1:34 (2002 issue) and 1:44 (older set),
# Friedrich Gulda 2:14 and Glenn Gould (1986 set listing) 2:08, both
# much broader. The conversion to a beat rate assumes the tempo
# relations used below, so it is approximate. 100 follows Hewitt and
# Tureck: driven, but every sixteenth stays distinct on the chip.
#
# Structure (from the Mutopia score; harmony read from the bass of each
# bar in the MIDI):
#   1-4    C minor over a tonic pedal: the figure stated.
#   5-14   a sequence with a falling bass (C, B-flat, A-flat, G) out to
#          the relative major, E-flat, reached at bar 14.
#   15-20  diminished harmony and a chromatic bass falling to A (20):
#          the darkest stretch, leading back toward C minor.
#   21-27  the dominant pedal on G. From bar 25 the figure breaks up
#          into broken chords passed between the hands over the held G.
#   28-33  Presto: the hands race in running sixteenths, still over G.
#   34     Adagio: a recitative in the right hand over two spread
#          chords, ornamental runs in thirty-seconds and sixty-fourths.
#   35-37  Allegro: a last flourish in the left hand over a C pedal.
#   38     the final chord, C major (Picardy third), with a fermata.
#
# Range: the chip's lowest note is about B2, so the bass of bars 7-11
# and the whole dominant pedal (G2, bars 21-29) is lifted an octave and
# sounds at G3, inside the texture; the terrace and ramp carry the
# weight the low pedal would give on a keyboard. The C2 of the close is
# lifted too.

tempo 100

voice mid 0          # two hands, one line each: no inner voice to set back

dyn 1 mf             # bars 1-14: the figure, out to E-flat
dyn 15 mp            # bars 15-20: diminished chords, the falling chromatic
                     # bass: a quieter manual
ramp 21-27 f         # bars 21-27: the dominant pedal, the one long build
dyn 28 f             # bars 28-33: Presto, full
broaden 28-33 1.3    # Presto: about quarter 130 (the MIDI's 160 against
                     # 120 is the same ratio, 4:3)
dyn 34 mp            # bar 34: Adagio, a recitative, softer and free
broaden 34 0.42      # about quarter 42 (the MIDI has 40)
dyn 35 mf            # bars 35-38: Allegro, the close over the C pedal
broaden 35-37 0.96   # Allegro: a shade under the opening tempo (the MIDI's
                     # 96 against 120 is slower still; Hewitt and Richter
                     # come back close to the opening speed)

rit 1.5 0.6          # from the middle of bar 37 into the final chord
